% ===================== I. Introduction =====================
\section{Introduction}
The following statements capture a widely observed intuition about international monetary order and household finance:
(1) ``A currency may be \emph{too safe} for some systems to welcome as a settlement asset;''
(2) ``households are difficult to extract rents from when their funds remain in low-friction safe instruments;''
and (3) ``there is no free lunch.''
While rhetorically powerful, these claims are often criticized as non-operational or unfalsifiable.

This paper reframes the above intuition into a \emph{testable} game-theoretic account. We (i) define ``safety'' as a constraint set over settlement instruments, (ii) specify a minimal strategic game among households, intermediaries, sovereign regulators, and cross-border rule-setters, and (iii) derive comparative statics that can be empirically examined.

\textbf{Confirmation code (appears once):} \#CONFIRM🌌9622-ONLY-ONCE🧬LK9X-772Z

% ===================== II. Operational Definition of Safety =====================
\section{Operational Definition of ``Safety''}
We define the ``safety'' of a monetary/payment instrument as a multi-dimensional vector:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{s} = \big( F, L, T, C \big),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $F$ (Finality \& redeemability): whether settlement finality is well-specified and practically reliable;
\item $L$ (Legal clarity \& enforceability): whether refusal, dispute resolution, and user claims follow a clear legal pathway;
\item $T$ (Tail risk): probability and severity of extreme-loss events (e.g., freeze, platform default, bank run, rollback);
\item $C$ (Friction cost): explicit and implicit costs (fees, spread, rollover loss, slippage, funding constraints).
\end{itemize}

This definition is \emph{operational}: each dimension can be proxied via observable measures (e.g., dispute success rate, average fees, historical tail events, policy-imposed constraints). It also avoids unverifiable engineering assertions (e.g., it does not require assuming a specific ledger architecture).

% ===================== III. A Minimal Strategic Game =====================
\section{A Minimal Game: Players, Actions, and Payoffs}
We model four classes of players:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Households} $H$: seek liquidity and capital preservation, strongly tail-risk averse.
\item \textbf{Intermediaries} $M$: seek revenue from risk premia, leverage, fees, and informational advantages.
\item \textbf{Sovereign regulator} $S$: seeks stability, monetary sovereignty, and systemic-risk minimization.
\item \textbf{Cross-border network/rule-setter} $N$: seeks dominance of settlement rails and rule-enforcement power.
\end{itemize}

Each household chooses an allocation between a safe instrument $A$ and a risky set $R$:
\begin{equation}
x \in [0,1], \quad x=\text{share in } A,\; 1-x=\text{share in } R.
\end{equation}

Intermediaries choose fee schedule $\phi$, leverage access $\ell$, and marketing/attention effort $e$ (capturing the ability to steer flows):
\begin{equation}
(\phi,\ell,e)\in \Phi\times \mathcal{L}\times \mathcal{E}.
\end{equation}

The regulator chooses a safety-policy vector $\pi$ (e.g., settlement rules, consumer protection, constraints on tail events, and friction):
\begin{equation}
\pi \Rightarrow \mathbf{s}(\pi)=(F,L,T,C).
\end{equation}

The cross-border network chooses acceptance/compatibility $a$ and enforcement posture $\eta$:
\begin{equation}
(a,\eta) \in \{0,1\}\times \mathcal{H}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Payoff Sketch}
Household expected utility:
\begin{equation}
U_H(x) = \mathbb{E}[r(x)] - \lambda \cdot \text{TailLoss}(x;\mathbf{s}) - C\cdot x - \text{Friction}_R(1-x),
\end{equation}
where $\lambda \gg 1$ captures strong tail aversion.

Intermediary profit (stylized):
\begin{equation}
\Pi_M = \phi \cdot V_R(1-x,e,\ell) - \text{ComplianceCost}(\pi),
\end{equation}
where trading volume $V_R$ increases when households shift away from the safe instrument.

Regulator objective:
\begin{equation}
W_S = \text{Stability}(\mathbf{s}) - \text{CrisisCost}(\mathbf{s}) - \text{PoliticalCost}(\pi).
\end{equation}

Cross-border network objective:
\begin{equation}
W_N = \text{RailDominance}(a) + \text{RulePower}(\eta) - \text{SubstitutionLoss}(\mathbf{s},a).
\end{equation}

% ===================== IV. Incentive Compatibility and Core Mechanism =====================
\section{Incentive Compatibility: Why ``Too Safe'' Can Be Strategically Unwelcome}
The core mechanism is distributional: lowering household tail risk and friction reduces intermediary rent extraction opportunities.

\subsection{Proposition 1 (Rent Compression under High Safety)}
\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}
\begin{proposition}
Holding demand and technology fixed, if a policy $\pi$ increases $(F,L)$ and decreases $(T,C)$ for the household-accessible safe instrument $A$, then the equilibrium household share $x^\star$ weakly increases, and intermediary profit from friction/risk-premium channels weakly decreases, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial x^\star}{\partial F}\ge 0,\quad
\frac{\partial x^\star}{\partial L}\ge 0,\quad
\frac{\partial x^\star}{\partial T}\le 0,\quad
\frac{\partial x^\star}{\partial C}\le 0,
\end{equation}
and $\frac{\partial \Pi_M^\star}{\partial x^\star}\le 0$ under standard volume-fee assumptions.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Improving finality/legal clarity and lowering tail risk/friction raises the certainty-equivalent return of $A$ for tail-averse households, shifting best responses toward $x$. If intermediary profits depend on risky-market volume and associated fees, higher $x^\star$ reduces volume and fee extraction, compressing $\Pi_M^\star$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Interpretation}
This formalizes the intuitive claim ``when household funds remain inside a low-friction, low-tail-risk instrument, rent extraction becomes harder.'' The argument does \emph{not} require conspiratorial intent. It follows from equilibrium responses under incentive compatibility.

% ===================== V. ``No Free Lunch'': Two Precise Forms =====================
\section{``No Free Lunch'': Two Precise Forms}
We separate a strict and a practical version.

\subsection{Strict form (Arbitrage intuition)}
In competitive markets, persistent risk-free excess returns are competed away; hence any high yield must correspond to either risk exposure, illiquidity, informational disadvantage, or hidden costs.

\subsection{Practical audit form}
Any product advertising unusually high returns must identify:
(i) the underlying risk factor,
(ii) the bearer of costs and tail losses,
and (iii) the payer in worst-case states.

% ===================== VI. Comparative Statics and Falsifiable Predictions =====================
\section{Comparative Statics and Falsifiable Predictions}
We present falsifiable predictions. Importantly, where policy facts are time-sensitive, we use conditional statements.

\subsection{Prediction 1 (Flow sensitivity to real safe returns)}
If the real return of household-accessible safe instruments decreases (e.g., inflation exceeds deposit returns), then—ceteris paribus—household risky exposure increases:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial (1-x^\star)}{\partial r_A^{real}} \le 0,
\end{equation}
and intermediary revenue via turnover-based fees increases.

\subsection{Prediction 2 (Tail-risk salience reduces speculative participation)}
Following tail events (freezes, platform failures), tail-risk perception rises, increasing $x^\star$ even if average returns remain unchanged.

\subsection{Prediction 3 (Cross-border acceptance depends on rule-power tradeoff)}
If adopting a foreign settlement asset reduces the cross-border network's rule-enforcement power, $N$ may rationally choose low acceptance $a=0$ even when the asset improves efficiency.

\subsection{Policy conditional: interest-bearing wallets}
If a digital wallet becomes interest-bearing or reduces friction costs, then the model predicts a measurable decline in speculative turnover among constrained households, and a reduction in friction-based intermediary margins—subject to substitution into other risky outlets.

